#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t)$. If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda$?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 3λe^{λt} - 4e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 3λ - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0$." :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + λe^{λx} - 2e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + λ - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0$, which of the following could **not** be $y(x)$?\n\n1. $-3e^{-6x}$\n2. $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n3. $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + 7λe^{λx} + 6e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + 7λ + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 7λ + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 6)(λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -6\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{-x}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x$, which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
